+A model may answer 4 because a variable is named \texttt{four}, even when its equations
+give 6. We ask whether writing the calculation helps it resist this misleading word.
+Building on \citeauthor{kudo2026faithful}'s arithmetic study, we introduce matched problems
+that preserve the equations while a variable's name suggests an incorrect digit.
+This separates answers following the name from other arithmetic mistakes. Across
+\NUM{n-models} models and three sets of worked examples, we compare prompting for just
+the final digit with chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting, which requests the equations and
+their evaluation first. Without CoT, renaming can change accuracy and draw answers toward
+the misleading word. With CoT, the rate of these answers differs by at most
+\NUM{eq-delta} percentage points between misleading and ordinary names in
+\NUM{eq-cot-equivalent} of \NUM{eq-cot-cells} comparisons. Linear probes predict the
+correct digit from internal activations in most model-variable pairs on ordinary-name
+problems with supplied correct calculations. The matched comparison shows that CoT
+usually improves resistance to misleading labels, making robustness to irrelevant words
+a measurable benefit of writing out the calculation.
 \end{abstract}
 
